          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My home blood pressure has been around 150/95 for two weeks. Is that something I need to act on now, and what do the guidelines say the target should be?",
          "answer": "**Urgency:** The symptom check found no emergency pattern in the cases it covers, and it rated this as routine. That is not a clearance. If you develop severe, worsening, or new symptoms that you can't explain, seek medical advice promptly.\n\n**Where these readings fall:** Under the US classification, office readings of 140 or higher systolic, or 90 or higher diastolic, are stage 2 hypertension. A reading of 150/95 falls in that range. European guidance uses 140/90 as the office threshold, so the label depends on which guidance is used. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*.) A diagnosis is not made from one reading. It needs an average of two or more readings on two or more occasions. Home readings are a recognized way to confirm this. (Same source.)\n\n**The target:** For most treated adults, US guidance describes a target below 130/80 mm Hg. Targets can be individualized for older adults, people with frailty or limited life expectancy, or people with many other conditions. (Same source.) Once treatment starts, guidance suggests reviewing about monthly until the target is reached, then every three to six months once readings are stable. (*Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*.)\n\n**What to do next:** Take your home log to a clinician and ask whether your readings are confirmed, what your target should be, and whether any treatment is needed. The retrieved guidance does not say whether your two-week pattern needs action this week, and it does not cover your personal risk. Do not start, stop, or change any medication based on this information. Lifestyle factors such as regular activity and lower sodium intake are also discussed in the retrieved material (*Physical activity for high blood pressure*; *Diet for high blood pressure*), but those sources do not give amounts for your situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\n---\n\n*Note on the rewrite: I changed one sentence. The earlier wording, \"Your readings would fall in that range,\" linked your numbers to a diagnosis. I've reworded it to describe the reading itself, because the system rules don't allow implying that you have a condition. Everything else is unchanged.*",
